--TEST--
A file whose first two bytes are `#!` is already in code mode, so an `<?nvs` before any `?>` is refused by name rather than by a lex error, and `<?php` is not an open tag in either mode (`rule:tooling/shebang-opens-code-mode`, `rule:statements/nvs-is-the-only-open-tag`)
--FILE--
#!/usr/bin/env nvs
// covers: lang:programs/code-mode-and-html-mode
// `rule:tooling/shebang-opens-code-mode`: line 1 is trivia and the file
// continues in code mode, so this statement needs no opening tag.
echo "one", "\n";

// The file is already in code mode, so the tag below opens nothing.
<?nvs
echo "two", "\n";
?>
<?php
echo "three", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0009]: this file opens with `#!` and is already in code mode
%A
error[E0229]: `<?php` is not supported
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
